Japan's export and import prices hit record high since 1979
Japan's exports and imports in December 2021 hit a record high against the yen as supply eased by the end of the year, data showed on Thursday. 2021 at a time of rising prices. According to Reuters.
However, the chronic shortage of semi-replicators remains a headache for Japanese companies such as Toyota, which cut their short-term production targets this week amid uncertainty surrounding the virus. New Omi City.
Exports rose 17.5 percent in December 2021 from a year earlier, higher than economists had expected, Reuters reported, citing data from the Japanese Ministry of Finance. According to Reuters, there was a 16 percent increase, but below the 20.5 percent increase achieved in November 2021.
In yen terms, exports hit an all-time high of 7,881.4 billion yen ($ 69 billion) and imports rose to 8,463.8 billion yen. The largest since the data was tracked in January 1979. This increase is largely due to rising inflation, which affects export and import flows.
In terms of value, steel exports increased by 75.1 percent in December 2021 from December 2020, but in volume, exports increased by only 10.2 percent, indicating an increase. Commodity prices pushed up Japanese goods sold abroad.
