The earliest method of making fireworks is to stuff the ammunition into a bamboo tube and then throw it into the fire, making a loud explosion. Later, the fireworks evolved further, with paper tubes being used instead of bamboo tubes, and they were no longer thrown into the fire, but paper clips were attached to them to ignite them.
By the 10th century, the Chinese had discovered that they could make explosives out of ammunition. So they attached fireworks with arrows that they fired at the enemy. During the next 200 years, firecrackers were transformed into missiles that could fire at enemies without the need for arrows. This technology is used in fireworks events.
Among European countries, fireworks are especially popular in Italy. During the 1830s, people in Italy began to think of a mixture of substances to enhance the brightness of fireworks and create many unique shapes. Finally, they were able to create new colors for the fireworks, while initially the fireworks had only one orange color.
