(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan, through its Embassy in Cambodia, has provided a total of US $ 728,501 to three Recipients under a grant scheme for a Japanese NGO project to strengthen the medical system for implementation. Pediatric surgery, strengthening community health care and child support networks, and improving the learning environment through the construction of primary schools.
The signing ceremony was held on January 19, 2022 by Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia and representatives of the three recipients.
In this project, the Japan Foundation aims to reduce under-five mortality and promote healthy growth of children. To achieve this, PHJ will continue its activities to support the training of medical staff and health volunteers, as well as to expand its network with villagers in the community.
PHJ will also provide nutrition education along with culinary training to increase the knowledge of villagers with young children. Through this project, 4,669 children under the age of five will benefit from enhancing their parents' knowledge, which will lead them to healthy growth in the future.
* Third: Project to improve the learning environment through the construction of school buildings in primary schools in Battambang province (budget $ 402,583) Recipient: Volunteer Organization (SVA)
The Japan NGO Grant Program has been in Cambodia since 2002 to support the activities of Japanese NGOs in their efforts to support the development and reconstruction of Cambodia at the grassroots level.
Since 2002, the Government of Japan has provided more than US $ 45 million for 138 projects focusing on basic education, health, agriculture and demining activities in Cambodia.
With this provision, the Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia has recently noted that Cambodia has been developing rapidly, but there are still some challenges that need to be addressed to improve the quality of life of the people, such as cutting Reduce poverty and social development, including health and education environment.
