Threats to the survival of mountain crocodiles include: degradation and habitat loss, illegal hunting caused by the need for illegal wildlife trade, illegal capture to supply crocodile farms, capture and breeding Breeding with other crocodiles, dams and illegal fishing.
In addition, a senior official of the Ministry of Environment said that the presence of a total of 10 crocodiles and mountain crocodiles at this time is a clear indication of the presence of a flock of mountain crocodiles, which have been recorded by scientists. Their first in September 2021.
"The discovery of the mountain crocodile herd is now the first evidence of the animal's survival after more than a decade of research in the Srepok Wildlife Sanctuary," he said. "It is a protected area of the eastern landscape forest area under the control of the Ministry of Environment and supported by WWF."
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment thanked the scientific research team of the Ministry of Environment and WWF for their hard work in studying and researching the results, with the first official record of the mountain crocodile being Reproduction and growth in Srepok Conservation Area.
Meanwhile, a spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said that the current record of the presence of mountain crocodiles underscores the importance of the Srepok Wildlife Sanctuary, the largest rainforest in Southeast Asia and A global biodiversity hotspot with high potential for biodiversity restoration and significant wildlife numbers of global value.
"The discovery of the mountain crocodile herd in Srepok now gives hope for the conservation and protection of this species in its natural habitat, which is very important," he added. For Cambodia and the whole world. ”
At the same time, Mr. Seng Teak, Director of WWF Cambodia, said: "We need to further strengthen protection and conservation efforts by prioritizing the search for appropriate and innovative solutions to support protected area management. "Nature and the protection of wildlife resources, and at the same time continue to call for better measures and methods to manage, use and manage natural resources."
The Ministry of Environment and WWF will continue to monitor the situation of mountain crocodiles in the Srepok area and strengthen measures to protect their habitats from threats such as land grabbing, encroachment and illegal activities. Others, by consistently carrying out regular patrols by all means, both inside and outside the protected area.
On behalf of the Ministry of Environment, Mr. Neth Pheaktra called on all stakeholders to take further joint measures for the protection and conservation of mountain crocodiles so that the world's rarest animals can avoid extinction and put in place measures. To ensure that the animal has increased in number to support its future survival.
The Ministry of Environment is ready to work with international non-governmental organizations, local communities and other partners to protect and preserve Cambodia's forest and wildlife heritage for long-term social and economic benefits. This generation and future generations.
He also called on people living around the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary and relevant authorities in Mondulkiri to protect and preserve the mountain crocodile.
