Thailand is expected to begin operations on the first phase of the 873-kilometer high-speed rail line connecting China with China in 2026, which is later than planned, a senior Thai official said on Tuesday. 3 years. According to the Bangkok Post.
The first phase of the railway connects Bangkok to Nakhon Ratchasima, which is about 200 kilometers long. The second phase connects Nakhon Ratchasima to Nang Khai, which borders Laos, at a distance of about 330 km.
The project is part of China's ambitious Belt and Road Initiative (BRI), which aims to connect China by land and sea to Southeast Asia, Central Asia, South Asia, the Middle East and Europe.
Thai and Chinese state-owned companies signed agreements worth 5.2 billion baht for the project in 2017, but were delayed due to disagreements over design, financing and technical assistance. According to the Bangkok Post.
