A senior official at the Ministry of Economy and Finance confirmed that the value-added tax exemption on basic food items was first introduced in 2018, when the state lost about $ 40 million in tax revenue, so for two years it was about 80. Million dollars.
Speaking to Fresh News on the afternoon of January 18, 2021, Mr. Nop Sothonvichet, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Economy and Finance, said that the Ministry of Economy and Finance recently announced the state's responsibility for the tax on Value added on basic food items for people's daily living for two more years from 2022 to 2023.
The issuance of this Prakas is a two-year extension of the value-added tax on basic food items as a burden on the state, which has been allowed since 2018.
2018 is the year to launch this incentive mechanism for the first time and see that the tax revenue from related sectors has decreased by about 40 million dollars, this is the contribution of the Royal Government to facilitate the daily life of the people and growth. Of related sectors.
With the care of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister and the Ministry of Economy and Finance have organized many tax incentives such as tax exemption in order to facilitate the lives of the people. And facilitate the affected enterprises or sectors or sectors that require high attention to drive growth and development in those sectors.
For the tax exemption, this is a measure that takes into account the daily life of the people, which in principle is the people who buy those foods to eat daily, which can buy from the supermarket market, the state will not charge tax (value added tax) Extra) on those basic foods.
In addition to the tax exemptions and incentives set by the Royal Government to facilitate this, the Ministry of Economy calls for more participation from enterprises and companies as taxpayers to pay attention to the implementation of tax obligations. Ati is obliged to register, declare taxes properly and avoid tax evasion.
Please be informed that the Ministry of Economy and Finance on January 11, 2022 issued a proclamation on the burden of the state on value-added tax on basic food items for people's daily lives.
According to the proclamation, this Prakas applies to the self-proclaimed taxpayers who supply the local food goods as the basis for the livelihood of the people in the Kingdom of Cambodia.
For the purpose of this post, "Basic Food" refers to the following items that are necessary for daily life:
* All kinds of pet eggs, whether they are fresh eggs or processed by the method of pulp or roasting
* All kinds of freshwater and trout fish, including lobster, shrimp, prawns, crabs and all kinds of shellfish, whether fresh or processed by staining or roasting
