SEOUL (Reuters) - North Korea announced on Tuesday that two of its missiles it tested on Monday were "strategic guided missiles". Presumably they could be short-range ballistic missiles. This was released by the AFP news agency on Tuesday morning, January 18, 2022.
For the fourth missile test for early 2022 on Monday, Pyongyang claimed that it was conducted to understand the effectiveness of these weapons and in line with the commitment of North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in North Korea. To further strengthen and expand the country's military capabilities for 2022.
North Korea's state-run KCNA news agency reported on Tuesday that "two guided missiles were fired from western North Korea and hit a perfect target on the island." One in the eastern seas of Korea. "The Academy of Defense Sciences has confirmed the accuracy, safety and operational efficiency of the weapons system in production."
In the wake of the fourth test, South Korea on Monday expressed deep regret, but Seoul remains committed to trying to engage with North Korea to resume dialogue. However, the South Korean military has also claimed that it has sufficient capability to detect and shoot down North Korean missiles, even though they are supersonic.
