Battambang farmers reduce corn cultivation to increase potato cultivation while Thailand imposes stricter conditions on corn imports from Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture said that most farmers in Battambang province have now reduced their corn cultivation by increasing their cassava cultivation to meet the Thai market, as almost 90% of both crops are exported. To Thailand, while Thailand has imposed new strict conditions for the import of corn from Cambodia.
"It does not depend on the Thai market. They need a lot. Farmers need it because they go in and out through the border into Thailand. They have their community to manage crops along the border. If Thailand orders their corn," he said. "Grow a lot of corn. If he orders potatoes, he grows a lot of potatoes, but generally more potatoes than corn."
He also said that the price of corn and cassava this year is not the same, with corn more than 6 baht or about 700 riel per kilo, while the price of potatoes is 690 riel per kilo for dried potatoes and fresh potatoes, the price is between two. Bottom to 2 and a half baht, which is about 300 per kilogram. At the same time, planting corn has a higher risk than cassava because growing corn carries the risk of water shortage at the beginning of the season, which leads to lower yields and more labor.
He said that in 2021, the province exported about 80% of corn to Thailand and about 20% for domestic consumption. He said most of the corn exports to Thailand were more informal than formal exports.
Pang Vanna Set, director of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that the corn crop in Banteay Meanchey, which covers more than 10,000 hectares with a yield of 4 to 6 tons, was completely exported to Thailand.
