China agrees with US to release oil from national strategic stockpile to curb rising prices (Video inside) (Beijing): China will release oil from its national strategic stockpile during the Lunar New Year February is part of a plan negotiated by the United States with other oil-using countries to reduce global oil prices. According to Reuters.
Sources familiar with the talks between the world's two largest crude oil producers told Reuters that China had agreed by the end of 2021 to release an unspecified amount of oil, depending on price levels.
"China has agreed to release more oil if it rises above $ 85 a barrel and less if oil is close to $ 75 a barrel," a source said without giving details.
The release of the oil stockpile by China will take place during the Chinese New Year, the source said. China will close major economic activities to celebrate the biggest annual festival from January 31 to February 6.
China's National Strategic Reserve Administration did not immediately respond to a request for comment from Reuters.
Reuters reported in November that the agreement to release oil stocks by China was the result of a series of talks held by US President Joe Biden's administration with major oil consumers after limited supplies. Pushed oil prices to a multi-year high.
