UK, India begin talks on free trade agreement after leaving EU
"Trade agreements with India's emerging economy provide huge benefits for British companies, workers, workers and consumers," British Prime Minister Boris Johnson said in a statement. "We are taking advantage of the opportunities provided by the emerging economies of Indo-Pacific countries to strengthen our role on the international stage."
Britain is trying to reach a new free trade agreement to help offset a sharp drop in trade with its largest trading partner, the European Union, due to its departure. Britain has already agreed on trade agreements with New Zealand and Australia, but the expected effects of the two agreements are projected to fade due to the negative effects of weakening EU trade.
For negotiations with India, the total trade volume at risk in the Anglo-India agreement was reduced by British trade with the European Union. In 2019, trade with India accounted for about 3% of Britain's total trade with the EU.
