Bank of Thailand worries over rising cost of living
BANGKOK: The central bank is concerned about rising living costs, while overall price stability remains a problem, the central bank governor said. According to the Bangkok Post.
He added, however, that rising energy and food prices could push up the cost of living at a time when the economic recovery is slowing.
According to the Bangkok Post, the Bank of Thailand has forecast that the overall inflation rate will fall below the central bank's target of 1.2 percent for 2021, 1.7 percent in 2022 and 1.4 percent in 2023.
He added that now is not the right time to raise wages because the labor market is still fragile, despite declining incomes and rising living costs. Sethaput said raising the minimum wage could affect the outlook for employment.
Crazy cow disease urges some Asian countries to suspend beef imports from Canada
The Food and Drug Administration of Canada said Tuesday that abnormal cases of rabies are rare and spontaneous, in contrast to normal cases of rabies caused by eating contaminated food. Affected cows are slaughtered on the farm to avoid prolonged animal suffering and not to eat meat for human or animal feed.
In November last year, Brazil suspended beef exports to China due to reports of malaria cases. The export suspension was made as part of a health protocol between the two countries. China lifted the ban three months later. According to Bloomberg.
