National Bank of Cambodia: Credit restructuring helps people and businesses to some extent from the Kovid 19 crisis as deposits in the banking system continue to rise
(Phnom Penh): According to the National Bank of Cambodia, from November 2020 to November 2021, 328,552 loan reorganization accounts amounted to approximately $ 5.5 billion, contributing to the people. And business to a certain extent during the 19 outbreak of Kovid. Meanwhile, deposits in the banking system continue to grow.
This is according to the report in the program of organizing the meeting to summarize the work results for the year 2021 and the work direction for the year 2022 of the National Bank of Cambodia, which was held on January 13, 2022 at the National Bank of Cambodia under the participation of Mr. Chea Chanto Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia.
Mr. Chea Chanto said, "The banking system has continued to meet the financing needs of the private sector, maintaining the sustainability of lending to all sectors of the economy, as well as continuing to allow banking and financial institutions to provide preferential credit restructuring. 370,785 accounts equal to 22.4 trillion riel or 5.5 billion US dollars have eased the burden on citizens and businesses to some extent.
He added that deposits in the banking system continue to grow, reflecting the strong public and investor confidence in the banking system.
Among the payment systems in operation, the blockchain system, which uses blockchain technology, serves as a payment platform that facilitates the transfer of users' money from one banking and financial institution to another in addition to customer payments. Go to the trader directly, safe, fast and low cost.
Mr. Chea Chanto stated that although the economy in 2021 is better than in 2020, which is supported by the recovery of the world economy, which has boosted exports and the effective implementation of the Royal Government's economic support measures. Controlling community-based epidemics and deciding to vaccinate about 90% of the population has allowed economic activity to resume.
The recovery of the Cambodian economy has also benefited from the stabilization of the riel and the riel, which has protected the purchasing power of the people and contributed to maintaining investor confidence. Inflation was kept at 2.9%, a manageable low.
