Russia is gradually withdrawing troops from Kazakhstan after a week of deployment
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's Defense Ministry announced on Thursday that allied forces of the United Nations Security Council (CSTO) were preparing to withdraw from Kazakhstan just one week after its deployment. This was released by the AP news agency on Thursday evening, January 13, 2022.
The Kazakh government has called on Russian-led CSTO security forces to crack down on insurgencies that have erupted since January 2, 2022 due to rising fuel prices. Demonstrations erupted in the major cities of the Central Asian country, reaching the capital, Almaty, the last hotspot.
The Kazakh government has interpreted the request for Russian-led forces to enter its territory as "due to an investigation that found outside forces to intervene to set the situation on fire." Almaty has declared the foreign allies to be terrorists who must be defeated.
According to a British government website, Amanda Milling arrived in Cambodia on January 9, 2022 for a two-day visit to strengthen bilateral ties while Cambodia holds the ASEAN chairmanship. This year 2022.
"Cambodia will be an important partner for the United Kingdom in 2022 as we seek closer ties with our ASEAN friends." This is a quote from Amanda Milling by the British Government website.
"Coming to Phnom Penh, my first visit this year, demonstrates our commitment to deepening relations and working with Cambodia to address a number of major regional challenges, including rehabilitation," she said. "From Kovid 19, resolving the crisis in Myanmar and climate change."
She is the first Minister from the ASEAN Dialogue Partners to visit Cambodia since Cambodia took over the ASEAN Chairmanship from Brunei in early January 2022, as well as since the United Kingdom gained the ASEAN Dialogue Partnership. In the year 2021.
The British government also confirmed that Amanda Milling met with Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, as well as Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and the new Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair on Myanmar, to discuss promoting cooperation. Working closer to the challenges and working together to continue the relationship between the UK and ASEAN as a whole.
According to a press release from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia, obtained by Fresh News, the United Kingdom is strongly committed to supporting Cambodia in organizing the 2022 ASEAN Summit, which was made through Ms. Amanda Milling on the occasion of the meeting. Discuss with Mr. Prak Sokhon on January 11, 2022.
On that occasion, Mr. Prak Sokhon also reaffirmed Cambodia's support for the "Global Ocean Alliance" led by the United Kingdom and its participation in the "High Ambition for Nature and Humanity."
