By 2021, Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone has achieved more than 2 billion USD in exports and imports, an increase of more than 40%
(Phnom Penh): Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone, which has more than 100 Chinese enterprises in 2021, achieved a total export volume of 2,234 million US dollars, an increase of 42.75% compared to the same period. In the year 2020. This is according to the announcement of the Chinese Embassy in Cambodia.
The Chinese Embassy said: "In 2021, facing the problems that occur in the context of the Kovid 19 virus, Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone, while vigorously preventing the Kovid virus, has also made efforts to increase production, promote the construction and improvement of level 2. "Try to push the momentum in this difficult time."
At the same time, the economic zone has continuously improved the structure of production and is included in the rubber tire project, which has a total investment of 300 million US dollars, about 1,600 local jobs.
Regarding this issue, Dr. Ky Sereywat, President of the Chinese Study Center of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, told Fresh News on January 13 that the export of Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone has increased due to orders in some countries. Decreased by the Kovid 19 crisis, which diverted the flow of orders to Cambodia.
At the same time, he added that the rubber tire factory project is an opportunity for Cambodia to reduce the sale of rubber products as well as create jobs and increase exports. With the entry into force of the RCEP, Cambodia will be able to attract foreign investors to invest in industries or tire factories or the food industry, a medium-sized industry that the RCEP focuses on.
He added, "Cambodia has an abundance of industrial crops, especially rubber trees, and most of our rubber we sell rubber to Vietnam, which, despite the government's ban, is still illegally exported to export. Going out illegally, we sell pure raw materials and go to Vietnam, which is refined into rubber. "Now that we have a tire factory, it is an opportunity to create jobs for our people and boost exports."
Sihanoukville SEZ has factories such as electronics, electrical wiring, car tires, car parts, bathroom accessories, parquet and board, office equipment, garment, footwear, bags and suitcases, and many other consumer goods factories. More. World Bank: Cambodia's economy to grow by 4.5% in 2022 and 5.5% by 2023
The World Bank report released on January 12, 2022 states that the economies of tourism-dependent countries such as Cambodia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Thailand are not expected to recover to pre-emergence levels. Kovid 19 until 2022.
Recently, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, issued a forecast that in 2022, Cambodia's economic growth could jump more than 5 percent after Cambodia reopens.
Samdech is optimistic about such economic growth as the growth of agricultural and industrial exports has improved, as well as the arrival of the Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement and the entry into force of the RCEP. On January 1, 2022, which makes it possible for Cambodia to strengthen the agricultural sector and export agricultural products to foreign countries, especially China.
