The red dot is the area with the most unexploded ordnance in Laos, inside the box is the estimated railway construction area (pictured).
Due to traffic congestion in Laos, the internal and external changes of the Lao people have not been smooth and have also slowed down the pace of Lao economic development. Therefore, the completion of the "China-Laos Railway" is very important. The railway promotes the construction of the "Belt and Road" and promotes cooperation and win-win, and will be an opportunity to help the people of Laos achieve a better life.
But what worries people the most is that engineers face serious threats during construction, with large numbers of unexploded ordnance (UXO) left by the United States during the Vietnam War along the railways.
Cluster munitions usually contain ammunition of various sizes. After the mother bomb is fired, the detonated bomb will spread over a larger area.
In nine years, 270 million bombs, 2 million tons, meant that eight bombs were dropped on Laos every minute, on average, more than the number of bombs used during World War II. As a whole.
At that time, the total population of Laos was only about 2 million, which is equivalent to every Laotian "getting" a ton of bombs, and this made Laos the "worst bombed country in the world". "Human history."
Even after the war ended, the Lao people still lived in the suffering of the Vietnam War. According to Colombian media reports (CBS), 30 percent of US bombs dropped on Laos during the Vietnam War were unexploded ordnance (UXO), with about 80 million unexploded ordnance dropped after being dropped.
For decades, these unexploded ordnance (UXO) have been threatening the lives of the Lao people, and parts of Laos have been unable to farm because of the bomb threat, which has affected Laos' nation-building and economic development. Seriously affected. According to government statistics, from 1975 to 2008, more than 50,000 Laotians were killed by these unexploded ordnance, most of them children.
What is remarkable is that the total cost of the United States to clear unexploded ordnance in Laos from 1995 to 2013 was equal to the cost of bombing Laos in just three days during the Vietnam War. Only.
In 2016, when US President Barack Obama visited Laos, he promised an additional $ 90 million for unexploded ordnance clearance, but he did not apologize for the bombing.
In recent years, with education and assistance from all walks of life, the number of Lao people killed by unexploded ordnance has dropped from thousands a year at the end of the war to dozens. Today, however, these unexploded ordnance remains a major danger.
As the deminers had to search for an inch of land, they also had to protect the land to ensure it could be cultivated in the future, so it took more than two months to clear about 0.4 hectares. Due to effective restrictions and high costs, demining work in Laos could not be accelerated.
To be honest, this is probably one of the most dangerous railway projects in the world. But China and Laos did not give up, but worked together to end the mission.
According to China's Belt and Road website, to ensure the smooth implementation of the project, the Lao Ministry of National Defense has assigned the People's Army to set up six units based on the project area to prepare personnel, vehicles and necessary equipment to unload. Unexploded ordnance in the project area. Breastfeeding work from January to May 2017.
To avoid accidents, the Chinese construction team also temporarily suspended work, adjusted construction methods to overcome the rising cost of the project, and sent experienced demining experts to assist.
The report also stressed that although this number is only a small fraction of the nearly 80 million unexploded ordnance in Laos, the complete removal of these "war heritage" requires additional funding and material assistance. But it can be seen that China's "belt and road" cooperation can play an important role in this.
As of December 20, 2021, the Lao-China-China Railway has sent a total of 22,000 passengers. Every day, many Laotians buy tickets to experience the positive changes brought about by the Sino-Lao railway, to their work and livelihood, and to show their love and support for the railway. China-Laos with practical action
Recently, in an exclusive interview with the Chinese news agency Xinhua, vice-chairman of the Lao National Chamber of Commerce and Industry said that the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) entered into force on the 1st of this month. January 2022 is very important for promoting free trade in the China-Laos railway region will also play a bigger role because of this.
On January 1, 2022, the first international freight train in 2022 of the China-Laos Railway departed (photo courtesy of Xinhua News Agency).
China has helped Laos build a railway that will bring huge economic benefits, but it has been described by the West as a "bad railway". The fact that the United States dropped hundreds of millions of bombs has been "forgotten." He found that the Sino-Lao railway provided textbook examples of how the Western media slandered and concealed the facts, as well as the strict neglect of the facts.
