Ministry of Economy Report: Consumer and Service Price Index in Phnom Penh Rises
(Phnom Penh): As Cambodia reopens its economy as the Cambodian economy begins to recover, the price index of consumer goods and services in Phnom Penh increased by 3.8% compared to the same month last year.
According to the report of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, obtained by Fresh News on January 11, 2022, said that in November 2021, when the Royal Government announced the reopening of the economy, the price index of consumer goods and services in Phnom Penh increased by 3.8% compared to the same month last year.
Another factor is the rising cost of raw materials for production, labor and logistics, which makes the price of each commodity in the market more expensive. At the same time, Cambodia is a major importer of consumer goods, thus leading to an increase in the price of goods such as energy, fuel, etc., which is based entirely on the international market.
He said that the rise in consumer prices has affected the family economy due to the Kovid 19 crisis, affecting their income, especially those with a fixed income such as civil servants and workers. At the same time, it hurts the economy.
"In terms of the workers, there are some side effects, so the only way to do this is to think about the livelihood or the necessities, and the non-essentials, we have to cut some costs to avoid complications. Other things that can happen eventually. Dr. Hong Vannak further explained.
According to the report of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, in November 2021, the average price of gasoline was 4,500 riels per liter, an increase of 7.1%, and the average price of diesel was 4,075 riels, an increase of 4.5% compared to October.
