Developing countries need to be prepared for the effects of US Federal Reserve tightening policy
The International Monetary Fund (IMF) says developing countries must be prepared for a US rate hike, warning that the Fed's move sooner than expected could hurt financial markets. And spur capital outflows and depreciation of foreign currencies. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports, citing a message released Monday by the IMF, that it expects strong US economic growth to continue, with inflation likely to moderate this year. The global lender plans to release a new global economic forecast on January 25.
The IMF said that a gradual tightening and well-informed US monetary policy would have little effect on developing countries, with foreign demand helping to offset the impact of rising financial spending.
But U.S. wage inflation, or the ongoing supply crisis, could push prices higher than expected and spur faster inflation expectations, prompting the US Federal Reserve to raise interest rates faster.
"Developing countries should be prepared for a period of possible economic turmoil," the IMF said, citing risks posed by faster-than-expected rate hikes and a recurrence of the epidemic. There is.
"A faster-than-expected US Federal Reserve rate hike could hurt financial markets and tighten global financial conditions," a senior IMF official wrote in the message. "These developments could coincide with declining US demand and trade and could lead to capital outflows and falling currencies in emerging markets."
