Commentary: "American Democracy" Becomes a Curable, Chronic Disease
January 6, 2022 marks the one-year anniversary of the unrest at the United States National Assembly. Until now, has the event at the US House of Representatives been a violent demonstration or a protest for justice for the general election? The Republicans and Democrats of the United States are still divided and in sharp conflict.
At the same time, the divisions caused by the events at the House of Representatives make US "veto politics" more fragile. Over the past year, issues related to people's livelihoods, such as law enforcement reform, gun control measures, and economic spending plans, have plummeted, while US social governance has been hampered.
Even more dangerous is the influence of events in the US House of Representatives on the "overthrow of the United States." A US referendum in late 2021 found that more than 34 percent of Americans found that Americans' acts of violence against the US government were "reasonable." The last few decades.
If, say, a year before the riots at the US House of Representatives revealed the true nature of the serious illness of "American-style democracy," then a year then all the horrible phenomena in the United States have proved that "America" has become a chronic disease for which there is no cure.
