Myanmar court finally sentences Aung San Suu Kyi to "4 more years" in prison
Rangoon: A Myanmar court on Monday sentenced Aung San Suu Kyi to four years in prison for three crimes, the latest in a series of charges against former civilian leaders. Staged a coup to oust him. This was released by the AFP news agency on Monday evening, January 10, 2022.
The 76-year-old Suu Kyi was found guilty by a Myanmar court of two counts of importing and possessing an illegal radio and one charge, according to an AFP source. Another case involved violating measures to prevent the spread of COVID-19.
The additional sentence carries Suu Kyi to up to six years in prison, including a two-year sentence in late 2021.
In December 2021, a Myanmar court sentenced Aung San Suu Kyi to four years in prison for inciting and violating COVID-19 prevention measures during the election campaign. But later, Myanmar's military government leader Min Aung Lan called for the sentence to be reduced to two years and Suu Kyi to serve her sentence under house arrest in Naypyidaw.
