(Phnom Penh): The impact caused by the Kovid 19 crisis has caused some Cambodians to fall into poverty due to declining incomes and job losses. However, through the cash support program during the fight against Kovid-19, many families have been able to cover their basic needs, such as food, water, electricity, and access to health care. Health centers and hospitals.
Prek Ta Kong community, located south of Phnom Penh, is a poor community of more than 60 families. Most of the people living in this area are working as construction workers, housewives and low-paid workers.
Phuong Ratana, a housewife whose husband is a construction worker and has many family members, said that her husband's salary from working as a construction worker could not cover his daily expenses, but fortunately The fact that her family receives a government allowance of 300,000 riel per month has helped her family to be less destitute.
Dr. John Narith said that the Royal Government of Cambodia has decided to continue to implement this special cash subsidy program until September 2022.
Theng Panhathun, the government representative in charge of planning at the Ministry of Planning, said that prior to the outbreak of Kovid 19, only about 2.3 million Cambodians lived in poverty, but the impact of Kovid 19 had Putting 700,000 Cambodians, or 2.8 million people, into poverty.
