Chinese online fleet operator to be under scrutiny when launching overseas IPO
BEIJING (Reuters) - A revised Chinese regulation requires companies to be online fleet operators with more than 1 million users under cyber security scrutiny as they seek to issue securities. First public venture (IPO) in a foreign market. According to the Xinhua news agency.
According to the revised regulations, key information infrastructure operators purchasing network products and services, as well as network operators operating data processing activities, will be subject to security scrutiny if their activities are carried out, Xinhua reported. Affect or may affect national security.
The new law says regulators will assess whether a company's IPO could affect the management or misuse of critical information infrastructure, core and critical data, or data volumes. Lots of personal from foreign governments or not.
According to a press release issued by the China Internet Space Administration (CAC), the cyber security review application could yield three results. Firstly, there will be no requirement to check, secondly, if the IPO does not affect national security, the publication is possible, thirdly, if the IPO is found to affect the national security, the publication This will not be allowed.
The statement also noted that the new measures are aimed at further ensuring Internet space and data security and protecting national security.
Claiming that openness is a fundamental policy of China, the statement said China has always supported domestic enterprises to rationalize the use of foreign capital markets in accordance with laws and regulations.
Amendments to the cyber security review regulations were made by 13 central agencies, including the CAC, following the promulgation of the Data Security Act.
A number of components to the national security risk assessment are also included in the new regulations, which take effect on February 15. According to the Xinhua news agency.
