Ministry of Water Resources and JICA sign agreement on National Irrigation Standard Project
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology and the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA) on the morning of January 5, 2022 officially signed an agreement to officially launch the technical cooperation project on standardization. National Irrigation System.
The signing ceremony took place at the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, chaired by Mr. Bun Hean, Secretary of State and represented by Mr. Lim Kean Hor, Minister of Water Resources and Meteorology, and Ms. Kame Haruko, Chief Representative of JICA Cambodia.
According to a joint statement between the two sides, the project will start operations in the second quarter of 2022. Despite the remarkable achievements of the past year that have supported the increase in agricultural productivity through the repair and construction of irrigation systems, the stability of sufficient water supply for irrigation still plays an important role. To increase agricultural productivity.
In addition, the need to ensure stability and adequate water for irrigation increased significantly as agriculture became the basis of livelihoods in many countries during the 1940 epidemic. Agricultural modernization needs to be promoted, in which irrigation also plays an important role in ensuring the stability and efficiency of irrigation water.
In this context, the project will set standards for two major types of construction: the headwaters and canals, as well as various artistic constructions, according to Japanese criteria. Standard criteria and standard manuals will be developed for general purposes related to planning and design work for small, medium and large irrigation systems.
This standard document will contribute to the construction as well as capacity building of officials of the Ministry and the Department of Water Resources and Meteorology through:
1: Save on construction materials 2: Reduce design costs 3: Ensure design consistency with other project layouts 4: Be reasonable on construction work 5: Support globalization Improve technology 6: Work by local builders and engineers 7: Develop budget and procurement plans for the restoration of irrigation and drainage systems.
According to the descriptions in those records, the Jiajing earthquake seems to be very different from other earthquakes. Records claim that mountains collapsed, floods and fires raged for days, while the landscape changed dramatically. The same record estimates that some areas may have lost as much as 60 percent of their population.
Although we can not be 100% sure that the estimates are accurate, the Jiajing earthquake can still be considered the deadliest earthquake ever to date due to the number of people. The death toll is far higher than in any other earthquake disaster.
The deadliest earthquake in the world was the 2004 Jiajing earthquake, which was triggered by a 2004 tsunami in the Indian Ocean. It has killed 230,000 people in Indonesia, Thailand, Sri Lanka, India and other affected countries.
