The five nuclear powers pledged to curb nuclear proliferation and avoid any nuclear conflict or war in a rare joint statement facing current tensions between the West and the United States. The East.
In a statement released earlier this week, the five nuclear powers and permanent members of the UN Security Council, including China, France, Russia, the United Kingdom and the United States, said: "We firmly believe that the proliferation of nuclear weapons must be prevented in the future. "No one can win a nuclear war and never let such a war happen."
The pledges of the five nuclear powers came after a review of the Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT), which has been in force since 1970, was postponed. A new date has been confirmed due to COVID-19. The latest review of the first NPT treaty is due on January 4.
In a message posted on his Facebook account, Prime Minister Prayuth said that Thailand has been affected by many problems caused by Kovid-19 and economic problems due to the country's blockades and austerity measures.
The Bangkok Post reports that Prayuth has ordered action to address these crises, which have helped people in need. "We are pleased that these measures have shown signs of easing the plight of the people," he said.
He said that the purchase of vaccines and vaccination targets have reached more than 100 million doses so far.
He added that the reopening of the country has restarted the economy, starting the process of economic recovery, which is very necessary for the nation. Confidence indexes rose across agriculture, industry and services, while investment also rose. The aid package has helped maintain public purchasing power and boosted the economy.
Mr Prayuth added that the Thai government was prioritizing household debt, which had risen to a crisis level. He said the Thai government hopes to resolve household debt through various means and mechanisms, including debt negotiation and debt restructuring.
The city-state economy will grow 7.2 percent in 2021, in line with the Singapore government's official forecast and a rebound, Reuters reported on Monday, citing preliminary data released on Monday. From a decline of 5.4 percent in 2020.
Singapore, one of the world's largest financial and transportation hubs and is often seen as a leader in global economic growth, has achieved a good economic recovery as governments remain Around the world, Kovid has changed its strategy, shifting from a Kovid-free policy to living with the epidemic.
Singapore's gross domestic product (GDP) growth is the highest since the country achieved 14.5 per cent growth in 2010 as the economy recovers from the global financial crisis.
The government has said it expects to achieve between 3 and 5 per cent GDP growth by 2022.
Singapore's gross domestic product (GDP) grew 5.9 percent in the fourth quarter of 2021, compared with the same period in 2020, the Ministry of Commerce and Industry said in a statement. According to a Reuters poll, analysts put the figure at 5.4 percent. According to Reuters.
