Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement and RCEP Agreement Entry into Effective January 1, 2022
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodia-China Free Trade Agreement and the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) entered into force on January 1, 2022.
The two agreements will be a major driver of greater trade in goods, services and investment, especially providing additional markets for hundreds of new items for export from Cambodia. Transfer of technology, knowledge, skills and new skills from foreign direct investment (FDI) and new jobs to the people.
He added that Cambodian exporters who are required to export goods to the two countries may apply for a Certificate of Origin (CO) at the Ministry of Commerce to obtain concessions in accordance with the agreement.
At the same time, the Chinese Embassy in Cambodia on January 1, 2022 posted a message congratulating the entry into force of these two agreements, which will benefit Cambodia and China.
The Chinese embassy posted the message that "the two agreements in 2022 arrive as scheduled on January 1, two good news for trade between China and Cambodia." The Free Trade Agreement between the Government of the People's Republic of China and the Government of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) entered into force on the same day. Harvest year 2022 has officially begun.
Please be informed that the Free Trade Agreement (FTA) is a mutual tax exemption agreement between one country and another on the agreed products. The FTA is not only a product tax exemption agreement, but covers many areas, including services, investment, trade facilitation, and the removal of non-tariff barriers.
For the free trade agreement between Cambodia and China, it is claimed that Cambodia will import more than 340 products, 95 percent of which will be taxed to 0 percent, and the remaining 5 percent will be down within 10 years or possibly next year. To 0%.
The Comprehensive Regional Partnership (AEC) Agreement has been signed by 15 countries, including the 10 ASEAN member states, and five other free trade partners - Australia, China, Japan, South Korea and New Zealand. 2012 in Phnom Penh.
Cambodia also plans to establish new free trade agreements with a number of key trading partners, including the European Union, Asia, India, Russia, Japan and Mongolia.
