The following is a report on the importance of implementing tourism safety measures for inland waterway transport: The Ministry of Commerce is preparing to list Kampot fish sauce as a GI-branded product as producers see export opportunities.
(Phnom Penh): Kampot fish sauce producers are gradually preparing to produce fish sauce in accordance with sanitary standards for export to the international market in the future, while the Ministry of Commerce is currently studying the possibility of registering Kampot fish sauce as a product. Upcoming Geographical Indications (GI)
He added that after the inclusion of GI products, there will be a wider promotion, which will make it easier to export Cambodian products to foreign markets.
He added that at present, the production of fish sauce has not been exported to foreign markets because it is still produced naturally, with an annual production of 100,000 to 200,000 liters.
Mr. Suon Vichea, Director of the Secretariat of the National Committee for the Management of Intellectual Property and Director of the Department of Intellectual Property, also told Fresh News that the team has now prepared procedures to place Kampot fish sauce as a geographical indication through information gathering and meeting. Meet with stakeholders such as producers, business associations, etc. to compile a charge book and establish a professional association.
Kampot fish sauce has been produced and famous since the 1960s during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era and was discontinued and lost its reputation during the Khmer Rouge era. Later, it was researched and re-produced in 1995 as a family, sold locally and to tourists, competing with the market for fish sauce imported from neighboring countries such as Thailand and Vietnam.
After the promotion and recognition of the delicious taste of Kampot fish sauce, this kind of Cambodian product has regained some of the local market. This was stated by the General Director of Echche Ngov Heng Kampot Fish Sauce Company.
He expects that when Kampot fish sauce becomes a geographical indication, it will capture more than 50% of the market share of imported fish sauce.
