WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden has reiterated his call for Russian President Vladimir Putin to ease tensions with Ukraine or face "severe economic sanctions" as the United States and its European allies seek a common response.
Biden's warning came during a more than 50-minute telephone conversation with Putin on Thursday. According to White House spokeswoman Jen Psaki, during the talks, Biden made it clear that the United States and its allies, including allies, would take strong action in the event of Russia using military force against Ukraine.
The telephone meeting between Biden and Putin was the second direct meeting this December in the context of Russia's reported deployment of nearly 100,000 troops near the region. Ukraine's border, and this activity has been accused by US intelligence officials of preparing to attack Ukraine in January or early February 2022. Moscow has denied the allegations.
Cambodia submits carbon-neutral long-term development strategy to UN Framework Convention on Climate Change
Cambodia is one of the 49 UNFCCC Parties that have submitted this strategy to the UNFCCC Secretariat to date, and is the second least developed country to submit this strategy with clear goals for carbon neutrality. In 2050. This is another testament to the strong commitment, cohesion and high political will to address climate change.
Although Cambodia has made little contribution to greenhouse gas emissions, through this strategy, Cambodia has demonstrated its willingness and position to engage with the international community to address climate change based on the principle of "shared responsibility, but at different levels." According to their respective abilities.
The strategy aims to provide a roadmap based on comprehensive analysis and scenarios related to key economic sectors, including priority mitigation activities to achieve future carbon-neutral development. This strategy includes an analysis of the balance between greenhouse gas reduction, economic growth, social justice and climate resilience.
Cambodia can achieve carbon neutrality by 2050 through the first: continuing efforts to implement the Reduction and Reduction of Renewable and Deforestation (REDD +) program and forest sector reform, including development Sustainable forestry: Second, reducing greenhouse gas emissions and transportation and improving energy efficiency; and third, promoting low-carbon agriculture, industrial processes and waste management.
