Singapore's economic growth slows in Q4 due to new virus
SINGAPORE: Singapore's economic growth is expected to slow in the fourth quarter, in part due to uncertainty caused by the omega virus, and analysts say the outlook for next year will be based on global economic progress. . According to Reuters.
Gross domestic product (GDP) is expected to grow 5.4 percent in the quarter, according to the median estimate in a survey conducted by Reuters. Fourth, compared to the same period last year. In the third quarter, Singapore's economy grew 7.1 percent.
The city-state's economic growth has been picking up this year as it suffers from restrictions related to Kuwait and the global economic downturn as countries around the world shift their strategies. Find a way to live with the coronavirus.
However, the tourist hub suspended ticket sales for flights and bus tickets to its country under a non-transferable travel program last week as the omega virus spread.
