The Iraqi prime minister said in a statement that "the international coalition's combat mission has been officially closed, with all combat troops and weapons withdrawn from Iraq." "According to the agreement in the current strategic dialogue, their role is limited to consulting and technical support."
On January 5, 2020, the Iraqi parliament passed a resolution requiring the Iraqi government to end the presence of foreign troops in the Middle East country. In July 2021, the United States and Iraq held a strategic dialogue and agreed to withdraw all US combat troops from Iraq by December 31, 2021.
The coalition launched an operation in Iraq against ISIS in 2014. Prior to the withdrawal, the United States kept a total of 2,500 troops in Iraq, while other participating countries kept about 1,000.
