Model Vegetable Safety Center Launched to Promote Safe Vegetable Chains to Good Agricultural Standards
(Phnom Penh): The Model Vegetable Safety Center and the Establishment of Partnership to Promote Safe Vegetable Chains in Accordance with Good Agricultural Practices (GAP) Standards were inaugurated on December 30, 2021.
The Center for Safe Vegetables and the Establishment of Partnerships to Promote Safe Vegetable Chains in Accordance with Good Agricultural Practices was born from the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding between the General Department of Agriculture and Tropicam on October 14, 2020. On Cooperation on the Development of a Model Vegetable Safety Center Using 6 Hectares of Land in Banteay Dek Agricultural Experiment Station
Mr. Nging Chhay, Government Delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, said that the inauguration of the Model Vegetable Safety Center is a new achievement of the General Department of Agriculture's efforts in response to program policies. Agricultural development related to the development of agricultural modernization and strengthening the value chain of vegetable crops.
He said that under the umbrella of peace, investment in agriculture over the past decade has brought about significant positive changes in technology, production, quality and market, making Cambodia prosperous. In all areas and achieve good economic growth, which has led to the improvement of people's living standards to improve gradually.
This has made people more able to afford their daily lives on food with higher demands for higher quality and safety standards. In 2021, the area under vegetable production will be about 69,000 hectares and the total vegetable yield will be about 800,000 tons.
However, due to a number of challenges in both production and post-harvest techniques, as well as the lack of quality assurance and safety systems, the Cambodian vegetable value chain is in a state of non-competitiveness. Still strong compared to neighboring countries. This is Mr. Nging Chhay's additional confirmation.
Mr. Nging Chhay stated that at present, Cambodia is not yet able to supply its own vegetables one hundred percent, which requires the import of additional vegetables to meet domestic demand. But the imported vegetables do not have a clear source and do not control the level of chemical residues that can affect the health of consumers.
He said that in order to improve the production and supply of vegetables to meet domestic demand with quality, safety and competitiveness, as well as to further expand the vegetable export market, we have been turning the crisis of vegetable supply imbalance. This is to become a new opportunity for Cambodia's agricultural sector.
Oknha Hun Lak, CEO of Tropicam, said that in the first phase of setting up the center, the company has used its own investment capital from the beginning of March 2021 until now. For about a month now, the company has been working hard to turn the site into a reality, despite the hardships caused by the weather and the Kovid-19 outbreak of the February 20 event.
Construction of washing, packaging and storage facilities for vegetables at appropriate temperatures, and the installation of automatic washing, treatment and packaging machines, along with various means of transportation. At the same time, the company also studied the cost of vegetable production in The net house will also guarantee a full year crop rotation.
The Minister of Agriculture said that the current event is really in response to the "public-private-development partners and communities" approach to contribute to increasing productivity, diversification and commercialization of agriculture in Cambodia.
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries encourages and cooperates with all private companies wishing to invest in agriculture, especially in the value chain of vegetable crops, to promote the production of safe vegetable crops to grow to meet the needs of The district has led to a reduction in imports of vegetables from abroad.
