Case 004 against Yim Tith officially closed
(Phnom Penh): Case 004 against Yim Tith has been officially closed by the Co-Investigating Judges of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) on 29 December 2021. This ordered that Case 004 be sealed and archived.
The Co-Investigating Judges' order comes just one day after the Supreme Court Chamber (ECCC) ruled against the International Co-Prosecutor's request to send Case 004 against Yim Tith to trial. .
In their warrants, National Co-Investigating Judges You Bunleng and International Co-Investigating Judge Michael Boulander referred to the Supreme Court Chamber decision of 28 December 2021 declaring that the case against Yim Tith was Finish. However, the Chamber did not formally order itself to seal and archive the case.
The two Co-Investigating Judges recalled that on 18 October 2021, Yim Tith's lawyer submitted a request to the Co-Investigating Judges to close and seal the case, which was no longer valid to the relevant level. Closing request.
National Co-Investigating Judge You Bunleng and International Co-Investigating Judge Michael Boulander said that based on the Supreme Court Chamber decision, the Office of the Co-Investigating Judge has jurisdiction to seal and archive the archives in accordance with the rules. Internal Article 69 (2) (b), which applies in the case of a Closing Order, which is not an Exemption Order in the ordinary context of Internal Rule 67 (3) (a).
Based on the above reasons, the two Co-Investigating Judges announced that the request to close the requested case was no longer valid and ordered that Case 004 be sealed and archived.
It should be noted that on 28 December 2021, the Supreme Court Chamber of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) decided to reject the International Co-Prosecutor's request to send Case 004 against Yim Tith goes to trial.
With a unanimous vote, the Supreme Court Chamber ruled that "the International Co-Prosecutor's Appeal on the Pre-Trial Chamber's failure to refer Case 004 to trial as required by the ECCC Legal Framework The ECCC is acceptable in the interests of justice.
The application is considered by a majority of Chamber judges who provide legal clarity and clarity, including:
