Thai tourists unlikely to benefit from New Year holidays and Chinese New Year
BANGKOK: Thai tour operators are losing hope of welcoming tourists during the Chinese New Year and New Year holidays, with the Thai Ministry of Public Health estimating the worst-case scenario of 30,000 cases of the virus. One day in the first quarter of 2022. According to the Bangkok Post.
According to the Thai Ministry of Public Health, if the Kovid-19 virus spreads rapidly and rapidly, the best scenario could see an average of 10,000 new infections by mid-February, but in the worst case scenario, the number of new infections could increase. Up to more than 30,000 cases per day. According to the Bangkok Post.
The epidemic in Thailand, when the virus was rampant, peaked in August with a record 23,418 cases.
On the other hand, tourism activities can return if most Omega patients are mild and the general condition is less dangerous than expected while people will continue their normal travels. Real help, but not fake donations to administer expired vaccines. "
Nigeria received more than 250,000 doses of AstraZeneca UK 19 vaccine from Europe in October 2021, but almost half of it expired by November this year. To allay the concerns of its people about the safety of vaccines, the Nigerian government was forced to completely eradicate the vaccines that expired last week.
In Africa, like Nigeria, the number of expired vaccines from the West is not small. According to World Health Organization statistics, in July this year, eight African countries were facing out-of-date Kovid 19 vaccines. Malawi and South Sudan, for example, have forcibly destroyed or abandoned tens of thousands of doses of the vaccine.
The "huge gap in vaccine distribution" is the biggest obstacle to combating the spread of Kovid 19 worldwide today. Providing assistance to needy low-income countries is an inevitable obligation and responsibility of the international community, especially large countries around the world.
