Letter to readers: A new chapter in Cambodia-Vietnam bilateral relations
Mutual respect, mutual understanding, mutual trust and mutual benefit that the leaders of the two countries have nurtured and developed are the core principles of maintaining relations. Bilateral friendship between the two countries.
Political trust and people-to-people contacts are the solid foundations of a relationship based on trust, which is continuously strengthened through practical cooperation. Over the past decade, the people of the two countries have benefited from the fruits of cooperation based on results in all fields and at all levels.
The state visit of Vietnamese President Nguyen Tan Dung to Cambodia from December 21 to 22 at the invitation of His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni is a new milestone in bilateral relations.
The visit sets a new direction to deepen our cooperation, strengthen our joint efforts to maintain peace and stability in the region, and create more opportunities for the people and strengthen the spirit of friendship and solidarity. Between the current and future leaders of the two countries.
Cambodia and Vietnam have constantly adjusted their foreign policy strategies to meet emerging realities and challenges. Nevertheless, our national interests and core principles have never changed. We work closely to maintain international peace and stability. Without peace and stability, we cannot develop our respective countries.
Parliamentary cooperation between the two nations has also achieved many important achievements and is gaining new momentum. Vietnam's financial assistance to Cambodia for the construction of the new National Assembly Secretariat is a new chapter in bilateral cooperation between the two legislatures. Parliamentary diplomacy is increasingly important for maintaining peace and stability, promoting all opportunities and strengthening international order based on international law.
As the old saying goes, "We can change friends, but not neighbors." Cambodia and Vietnam have become increasingly interdependent. Our security and economic interests are increasingly intertwined. In this context, we need to support each other to protect our respective core interests. More interdependence leads to more opportunities between the two countries.
Therefore, we need to build the foundation of mutual security and prosperity, constantly bring more results for the people and put trust and people in front of and at the center of our relationship.
Finally, we need to invest more resources in promoting educational and cultural exchanges, especially between the young leaders of the two countries. I personally believe that people are the most important factor and role in international relations. Conquering the hearts of the people is the most important mission of diplomacy and foreign policy. Cultural relations and people-to-people form a bridge of trust between our two nations.
While the current generation of leaders, especially between the leaders of the ruling Cambodian People's Party and the Communist Party of Vietnam, have strong sentiments and personal ties, we need to do more to ensure that the next generation of leaders continue to maintain friendships. This important tradition and meaning.
We must also ensure that the legacy of mutual support and assistance for the two countries' historic quest for sovereignty and independence continues to be nurtured and nurtured for future generations.
Phnom Penh, December 28, 2021 Mr. Sous Yara, Member of the National Assembly from the Cambodian People's Party
