Ministry of Labor: Results of today's meeting on NagaWorld labor dispute, the two sides still do not agree to end the dispute
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training on the afternoon of December 27, 2021 continued to coordinate and resolve labor disputes at NagaWorld Limited.
The mediation was led by Mr. Tes Rukhaphal, Secretary General of the General Secretariat of the Commission for Resolving Strikes and Demonstrations in All Matters, with Ms. Chhim Sithor and Mr. Horm Sunrithda representing the 357 former workers who have not yet agreed. In accepting the severance pay, the contract was terminated and Mike Ngai represented the company.
According to the announcement of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, as a result, both parties have not yet agreed to end the dispute, with the remaining workers remaining from the agreement to accept compensation to terminate the contract in accordance with the law. Requiring the company to accept the return to work, while the company still maintains the position of not accepting the return to work by agreeing to pay compensation in accordance with the law.
As of today, the Ministry has tried to mediate with the representatives of the former workers and the representatives of the company five times after the protest on December 18, 2021, but still did not agree to end the case. Dispute still.
According to the Labor Law, such collective labor dispute resolution procedures must be mediated by the Labor Inspector, and in the event of non-settlement, the dispute will be referred to the Arbitration Council. If a party refuses to comply with an order of the Arbitration Council, the parties may appeal to the competent court to request that such order be exercised. In the event of a dispute requesting a strike, the court's procedures and decisions shall be followed.
In order to participate in the protection of the legitimate interests of former workers and companies in dispute, the Ministry of Labor will continue to try to mediate and resolve this case in accordance with the proper procedures of the law with the former workers and companies at the Ministry.
The Ministry of Labor continues to call on former workers as well as employers to cooperate with the Ministry and the competent authorities to end this dispute, to absolutely avoid any action that leads to harm to the interests of both parties and to suffer. The legal consequences eventually.
