Farmers complain about Vietnamese authorities closing Koh Roka border checkpoint in Prey Veng province to prevent them from transporting rice for sale
(Prey Veng): Farmers living along the road to the Koh Roka border checkpoint have complained that Vietnamese authorities have closed the Koh Roka border crossing, making it very difficult for them to farm because of the time. At harvest time, do not know where to sell.
The people of Peam Chor district have asked the district authorities to help solve the problem with the Vietnamese side to reopen the Koh Roka border checkpoint so that they can transport rice to sell in Vietnam as before.
Their farming business when reaping for sale is dependent on the Koh Roka border crossing because it is close to home, easy to transport, low cost. If the Vietnamese authorities refuse to reopen the Roka border crossing, they will be forced to transport rice to sell at other ports, and the cost of transportation will be higher. Difficult and affect their lives to another level.
Regarding the request of the people, Mr. Seng Thea, Governor of Peam Chor District, said that the leaders of the Koh Roka International Border Crossing are working hard to resolve the negotiations with the provincial leaders of Vietnam, but have not yet received results from Before, we negotiated at the border between the two provincial governors, Prey Veng and Don Thap, as if waiting for 2022, and this information has not been received yet, we are waiting for our commission to negotiate again.
However, the farmers also requested the relevant authorities in both provinces (Prey Veng and Don Thap) to expedite the settlement and reopen the Koh Roka border crossing because of their rice. It is also close to the upcoming harvest. Private sector in Takeo province calls on people to be careful of new omega virus
