(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia plans to launch the implementation of the "Cambodia Garment and Footwear Development Strategy 2021-2025" with the vision of developing this sector into an environmentally sustainable, durable, high value-added industry. Focus on high-value and unique products, highly competitive and fundamentally support economic diversification.
The Strategic Framework states that the garment sector, which generally includes footwear and bags, has developed continuously for the past three decades and has continued to contribute to the achievement of several major socio-economic achievements, including: economic growth, exports; Outreach, investment, poverty reduction and job creation.
However, the sector has been affected by the Kovid 19 crisis, coupled with structural problems that have slowed the sector's growth. Overall, the sector remains a low value-added manufacturing hub, and industry integration supporting other processes in the supply chain and value chain remains low.
In addition, the prolongation of the Kovid 19 crisis to the present has caused uncertainty in the garment sector, especially in Cambodia's major markets.
On the other hand, the situation in which Cambodia plans to move out of the status of a least developed country in the near future will cause Cambodia to gradually lose the benefits of the preferential trade system, thereby affecting the garment and manufacturing sectors. More Cambodian clothing, which used to rely on a preferential trade system for exporting goods to major international markets.
These challenges and risks require Cambodia to consider and address and be ready to seize opportunities to promote the development of a stronger and more resilient sector. Mr. Hang Sitha sends congratulatory message to Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh and Lok Chumteav Kittisangkhab Dr. Men Sam An, who were elected Vice-Chairman of the 43rd Central Committee of the Cambodian People's Party
