Ministry of Health and partner organizations organize a workshop to disseminate the results of the final evaluation of the provincial and primary eye health improvement project and the meeting of the eye health subcommittee
The workshop was attended by representatives from the Department of Health and Women's Affairs of the target provinces, including: Kandal, Kampong Chhnang, Sihanoukville, Kampong Thom, Kampong Speu and Prey Veng, the Disability Action Council, as well as partners from eye health partners and related institutions. A total of 65 people (70% of whom are women and 10% are people with disabilities).
The workshop was chaired by Prof. Dr. Ngy Meng, Advisor of the Ministry of Health and High Representative of the Ministry of Health and Mrs. San Arun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Women's Affairs.
Dr. Min Sokunthea, Deputy Director of Preah Sihanouk Provincial Health Department, warmly welcomed and welcomed all the delegates and distinguished guests who came to complete the mission and showed their full support for the eye health project of the partners who wish to help. To the people of Sihanoukville.
Ms. San Arun also made meaningful and substantial recommendations on the progress of eye health and clearly reflected that the Ministry of Women's Affairs has made significant contributions and actively provided interventions on eye health at both the national and sub-national levels and on behalf of To the Ministry of Women's Affairs, she welcomes the full support and good cooperation of all partners and relevant institutions that can promote better eye health.
This provincial and primary eye health promotion project aims to reduce avoidable blindness in Cambodia and aims to integrate the implementation of eye health programs into the Ministry of Health's primary health program.
Through the health information system, 38,318 people (22,377 women) have access to community-based screening and treatment services. 1,093 people (648 women) underwent cataract surgery and 377 people (234 women) underwent other eye surgeries through World Eye Health Day And International Women's Day.
(Phnom Penh): Cement imports in the first 10 months of 2021 decreased by about 33% compared to the same period in 2020, while steel imports for the construction sector increased by only 1% due to the decrease in activity. Built in the country in 2021.
According to a report by the Ministry of Commerce, from January to October, Cambodia imported about 40 million US dollars worth of cement, down 19 million US dollars or 33% compared to the same period in 2020, which amounted to 59 million US dollars. Million US dollars.
Steel imports in the first 10 months were valued at 326 million US dollars, up 1 percent from 321 million US dollars last year.
Put Chantarith, Secretary General of the Cement Industry Association of Cambodia, confirmed to Fresh News on December 23 that the decline in cement imports was due to a decline in construction activity before the expansion. Kovid 19 outbreak, while local companies' cement production to meet demand in the construction sector was about the same as last year, about 8 million tons.
"Our production remains the same, especially as imports fall due to construction declines due to the global Kovid 19 problem." Mr. Put Chantarith added.
Most of the cement and steel imported into Cambodia comes from Vietnam and Thailand.
Please be informed that Cambodia currently has 5 cement companies, including Kampot Cement Company, Chip Mong Inc. Cement Corporation, Chakrei Ting Cement Company, Battambang Consignment Cement Company and Thai Boon Rong Company Cement Company. Distorted by political drama
