Please read the announcement of Electricite du Cambodge as follows: This morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen inaugurated the Administration Building of the General Department of Customs and Excise.
(Phnom Penh): The 35-storey Administration Building of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia was officially inaugurated on Thursday, December 23, 2021 under the high presidency of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
The administrative building of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia began construction in February 2017 and was completed in July 2020. It is 35 stories high (excluding 2 basements), 120 meters high, 54 meters long and more than 26 meters wide.
In addition to the inauguration of the administration building, tomorrow's program also includes the inauguration of the National Customs Hall and the Customs Museum. Construction of the National Customs School began in October 2019 and was completed in October 2021. This building is built on an area of 10,026.31 square meters in the OCIC development area, Sangkat Prek Ta Sek, Khan Chroy Changva, Phnom Penh.
The Customs Museum building was renovated in August 2020 and completed in November 2020. The building is 38.70 meters long and 18 meters wide.
The new presence of the Cambodian Customs and Excise Administration Building not only enhances the image of state institutions and provides a suitable workplace for civil servants. But it also shows the progress of the Cambodian nation under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen in leading the nation-building out of the difficult situation after the remnants of the flames of war.
Please note that the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia is a public administration under the Ministry of Economy and Finance, which is an important institution in raising revenue to support the national economy.
The General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia was established during the Sangkum era by the Royal Kram dated June 29, 1951. The operation of the General Department of Customs and Excise was suspended from 1975 to 1979 under the Khmer Rouge regime.
The unit resumed operations on July 13, 1979 under the auspices of the Ministry of Commerce. However, it was transferred under the supervision of the Ministry of Economy and Finance through a sub-decree dated March 11, 1988. The Customs and Excise Office was established as the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia (ACC) through a sub-decree dated September 15, 2008.
The General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia is the staff of the Ministry of Economy and Finance with the following roles and responsibilities:
* Propose policies related to international trade exchanges, draft regulations related to the collection of taxes and duties on all types of exports and imports. In particular, consider the construction and implementation of customs tariffs and procedures related to customs and excise work on all types of goods in accordance with each stage of national and international economic development.
* The only leading body responsible for the inspection of export-import goods at the border and has the duty to facilitate a single joint inspection in case of inspection by the institution. Others in line with risk identification
* Manage, control and implement the collection of taxes, duties and other fees on all kinds of export-import goods, take measures to prevent and arrest all kinds of customs duty-free goods.
Participate in the preparation of the annual national budget to be included in the draft law on finance for each annual management, perform other duties as determined by the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
The General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia has a nationwide unified management system and has the right to direct and control all Customs and Excise units from the central level to the local level of the capital-province as determined by the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
The General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia has the following missions: To be responsible for managing, inspecting and collecting taxes, duties and other fees on exported or imported goods in accordance with the provisions of the Customs Law as well as other relevant laws and regulations. And export-import, circulation and warehousing.
In order to raise revenue to support the national economy and serve the needs of the Royal Government, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia has made efforts to reform and implement its roles and responsibilities consistently on its roles and responsibilities, especially the collection of taxes on export-import products.
According to the report of the Director General of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, 2019 was the year with the best revenue collection, achieving about 3.214 million US dollars, which is 142.3% of the revenue plan set by the Financial Law. Objects for management 2019.
In particular, for the five years from 2014 to 2019, the growth rate of taxes and duties collected by the General Department of Customs and Excise on average annually was about 19.5%, making the figure of revenue in 2019 almost doubled. Revenue collected in 2014.
But since the beginning of 2020, the Kovid-19 outbreak has had a serious negative impact on the global economy, including the Cambodian economy, and has severely shaken the international trade supply chain. This has significantly reduced the revenue collected by the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia.
In fact, in 2020, tax revenue and taxes collected by the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia will reach only 9,798.4 billion Riels, which is about 83.5 percent of the plan set by the Financial Law for 2020.
In 2021, revenue collected by the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia is estimated to reach 9,283 billion riels, or 97 percent of the plan set in the Financial Law for 2021 management.
