Chinese tax authorities order celebrities and online sellers to check their own tax mistakes and come out before the end of 2022
Viya, who sells live products via social media or e-commerce, originally named Huang Wei, was fined Monday by the Hangzhou Tax Office, Reuters reported. For concealing personal income and other crimes committed from 2019 to 2020.
On Wednesday, tax officials from China's most economically prosperous cities and provinces posted a notice on their website ordering celebrities and online sellers to exercise self-censorship. On errors related to its tax, and if it finds its own fault, correct those errors and report to the relevant tax authorities.
"Before the end of 2022, correct tax-related issues and report them to the tax office (which) will reduce, reduce or waive tax penalties in accordance with the requirements of this notice," the statement said. "
"If self-inspection and self-correction continue to be denied or neglected, the tax office will work in strict accordance with laws and regulations," the statement said.
The implementation of the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) will help create a new wave of growth for China's cross-border e-commerce sector. According to experts and those in the industry.
Signed in November last year among the 15 countries in the Asia-Pacific region, including China and 10 ASEAN members, the agreement will enter into force on January 1, 2022 for the 10 signatories: Brunei, Cambodia, Laos, Singapore. Thailand, Vietnam, China, Japan, New Zealand and Australia.
The cost of locally sourced raw materials for Chinese enterprises will be greatly reduced under the trade rules of this agreement, especially the rules on the origin of goods, which require only 40% for goods of origin. In member countries, the RCEP is much lower than other free trade agreements.
Industry experts say the RCEP agreement will facilitate cross-border e-commerce enterprises in expanding their presence in markets that currently impose higher tariffs on Chinese goods.
Wei added that it will make it easier for small and medium enterprises, especially private enterprises in China, to expand globally.
Wang cross-border e-commerce association executive chairman Wang Zhenchen added that cross-border e-commerce has great growth potential in areas such as electronics, clothing and toys in the Pearl River Delta, sports equipment, electronics and accessories. Cars and other products.
