WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden has banned non-humanitarian aid to North Korea for fiscal 2022, accusing it of committing human trafficking, the White House has said. This is according to the Yonhap press release on Wednesday, December 22, 2021.
The ban will remain in place until the governments of the countries that set it meet the "minimum standards" or work hard to bring them up to the minimum standards. The minimum standard was set in the United States as the 2000 Trafficking Victims Protection Act. The ban will also apply to officials in Cuba, Russia, Syria and other countries.
The State Department released its annual report on human trafficking in July, defining North Korea as a country that has committed human trafficking for 19 consecutive years and has not fully met "minimum standards". For the elimination of trafficking.
