Economist: End of February 20 event is a bell to confirm the economic process and business return to normal
(Phnom Penh): Economists and businessmen in Cambodia say that the announcement of the end of the event on February 20 will make business people more stable in doing business, which will promote better business and encourage Cambodia's economic growth next year, despite the recent discovery of a new mutant virus, Omikrong.
Dr. Hong Vannak, an economist at the Royal Academy of Cambodia, told Fresh News on December 21 that the announcement of the end of the February 20 event was a wake-up call for stakeholders, especially investors and business owners, to do business with confidence. And is to announce to foreign tourists that Cambodia has a good control over the spread of Kovid 19 disease, which makes their travels safer.
"This point can also be a message to investors or all business people to run their business in good spirits, not to panic too much," he said. "The effects of Kovid's disease on our family or social economy are growing steadily."
February 20 is a bitter event in Cambodia, causing more than 3,000 people to be infected and killed by 19 thousand Kovid diseases, in particular, greatly affecting the lives, businesses and the economy of Cambodia, and this effect has had an impact so far. To date, too.
He stressed that after the closure of February 20, the economic process will return to normal and will further boost economic growth next year as Cambodia implements new investment laws and free trade agreements with China, as well as the GSP and EBA.
"I believe that Cambodia's economic growth from next year will be even stronger if the opening of the economy goes smoothly, we will end this 20 February event and I believe we will have "There are more opportunities to find more investors to come to our country."
While the number of infections has dropped to less than 10, the discovery of a new mutant virus has also raised concerns that Cambodia has now identified four new mutants.
However, the two economists believe that the discovery of the new mutation will not disrupt Cambodia's business and economy, as Cambodia has good experience in managing Kovid 19.
Mr. Oknha Lim Heng said that according to the monitoring of the new mutant virus Omi Krong, if the spread of similar to the old or alpha will not have serious risk and will not seriously affect the Cambodian economy and do not interfere with market opening Cambodia's economy is not recovering.
Dr. Hong Vannak said that because Cambodia has experience in controlling the spread of Kovid 19, especially vaccination to almost 100% of the population, the case of Omi Krong will not have a significant impact on the business of industrial enterprises. No other production chain.
However, even if the current number of people infected with the new virus is small, a small amount of negligence can lead to a major event, especially if there is a community outbreak that could lead to a new community event. Another poses a high risk in the economic recovery.
