Philippines rushes to aid to typhoon-hit areas as death toll rises to 375
MANILA (Reuters) - Philippine forces on Tuesday were working hard to bring relief supplies, food and water to areas devastated by a devastating typhoon that left hundreds of thousands homeless. This was released by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, December 21, 2021.
The Philippine National Disaster Management Agency says more than 400,000 people have been sheltering in shelters set up by authorities or staying with relatives after their homes were damaged or destroyed. Completely by that strong typhoon.
A total of thousands of Philippine police, police and coastguards have been mobilized to help bring food, water and medicine to those in need. The Red Cross is also involved and has called for $ 22 million to be used in relief and rehabilitation efforts.
At least 375 people were killed and hundreds injured after Typhoon Rai hit the southern and central parts of the Philippine archipelago on Thursday.
