(Ratanakkiri): Even in the context of Kovid 19, "On Channa" is still cooperating with Banlung Market Investment Company to complete the construction of 4 regional stalls between January and early February 2022.
According to some vendors who are temporarily sitting in the shop, even though there are not many visitors in and out every day and the sales are not so good, they have the right place to shop, not in the sun, in the rain or in the wind. Under no umbrella.
They said that expenses such as taxes and sanitation cost 1,500 riel per day. They hope that when the management of Banlung Market arranges a place for traders to come together, that is, not to sell on public roads like now, the number of visitors to and from Banlung Market has increased, so they can sell more.
On Channa Company Representative Chuon Sophal, who is busy building stalls in Area 4, told Fresh News on the morning of December 21, 2021 that in order for traders to have the right place to sell according to the standard, the team cooperated with the investment company. Banlung Market provides infrastructure such as: pouring concrete, drainage system, roofing to be hygienic, orderly, environmentally, so that they do not face problems such as rain, wind, twitching like before.
The representative of "On Channa" company added that between January and February 2022, the construction of stalls in 4 areas will be completed and all vendors will not be allowed to sell in front of the market on the national road because it Affect the order and beauty of Banlung.
In addition, Mr. Chuon Sophal requested all traders who do not have a stall to contact the market office to meet with the market management to avoid receiving vague information such as: ID card, family card, residence book, etc. . The rental price of the stall is determined in the charge book of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
It should be reminded that after Mr. Nhem Sam Oeun took office as the Governor of Ratanakkiri Province, he visited and talked with traders in Banlung Market and promised to solve the challenges encountered in the past.
The AIDS Health Care Foundation Organizes a Reunion for a Project to Promote Girls in Poor and Difficult Communities
(Kampot): The AIDS Health Care Foundation (AHF) team and NGOs and community stakeholders have teamed up for a project to raise awareness of girls living in poor communities. Put and work with partners to meet the needs of girls who are vulnerable and in need of help.
The girls' promotion program was held last weekend in Kampot province at a resort in Kampot province, led by a team from the AHF and NGOs, including girls. Too many.
The program focuses on girls, girls who drop out of school, and young girls who live in poverty and are affected by peer pressure, leaving paid work, and for young women who are involved in relationships. Sex with a partner to reduce unplanned pregnancies, HIV infection and marriage of young girls.
Lisa, a girl leader, shared her experience in the community. She faced stigma and discrimination in the community, so she decided to drop out of school and become a gangster because of other friends. But now she realized after learning about the Girl Act project and becoming a girl leader. Atmosphere and support for girls is very important in promoting girls' education and good future.
Chanthy, a community leader for Girl Act, said she had a dark past because of AIDS, making her life difficult for herself and her family for a long time. But now she has become a leader in a community struggling for her future and a stable family.
Chanthy added that she does not want to see the next generation of HIV-positive people like her, they must be free of HIV and she is trying her best to promote girls with the utmost determination and care. .
Cambodian Youth Party announces 7 policies to compete in 2022 Commune / Sangkat Council Election
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of December 21, 2021, the Cambodian Youth Party announced the use of seven policies to compete in the 2022 Commune / Sangkat Council Election.
The launching ceremony of these 7 policies was held in the presence of Mr. Pich Sros, President of the Cambodian Youth Party, as well as some other leaders at the party's headquarters in Phnom Penh.
These seven policies include:
First, open a public forum once a month with the people to solve problems in the commune.
Second: Clearing traffic and drug use in communes.
Third: Buy an ambulance to transport sick people in the commune to the hospital free of charge.
Fourthly, the homeless elderly from the age of 60 onwards receive an allowance of 100,000 Riels per person every month and receive care from the commune / sangkat administration.
Fifth, strengthen agricultural production to get high market value.
Sixth: Open multi-skilled training courses for young people who drop out of school in the commune to have a clear livelihood.
Seventh: Increase the budget of 600,000 USD for commune development per year and strengthen the transparent use of the budget to accelerate the development of infrastructure in the commune / sangkat.
