KUALA LUMPUR: Floods triggered by heavy rains on the Malaysian peninsula last weekend are further evidence of the worsening effects of climate change as the Malaysian government considers That it was the worst flood in 100 years. This is according to the release by CNA on Tuesday morning, December 21, 2021.
The Malaysian government claims that the floods are unprecedented in the last 100 years and in 2021 we have already seen similar events in Germany, Turkey. , China, India and the northeastern United States, with New York the worst hit.
Earlier this week, the Malaysian Ministry of Water Resources and Environment claimed that the low pressure system had caused 24 hours of rain in the states on the Malaysian peninsula, and that the rainfall was equal to The amount of rain that falls in a month. Several provinces in Thailand bordering Malaysia have also been hit by floods.
"The rainfall for Kuala Lumpur is 2,400 mm per year, so it means that last weekend's rainfall was equal to one month's rainfall," the ministry said. "It was beyond our expectations and it always happened once in hundreds of years."
