Japan provides nearly $ 300,000 grant for school building and irrigation rehabilitation in rural Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan on December 20, 2021, through its Embassy in Cambodia, signed a grant of US $ 280,378 for the construction of school buildings and the rehabilitation of irrigation systems to a number of recipients. 3 Under the Grant Scheme for Small-Scale Human Security Project (Kusano Grant).
The grant agreement was signed in the presence of Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia and representatives of the three recipients to implement the following projects:
First: Project to build a school building at O'Yadao Secondary School in O'Yadao District, Rattanakiri Province (budget $ 98,317) Recipient Unit: Department of Education, Youth and Sports, Rattanakiri Province.
Second: Construction of school building at Orussey Leu 1 Primary School and Orussey Leu 2 Primary School in Siem Bok district, Stung Treng province (budget $ 91,566) Recipient: Department of Education, Youth and Sports, Stung Treng Province
Third: O'Sambour Irrigation Rehabilitation Project in Kampong Speu Province (US $ 90,495) Recipient: Department of Water Resources and Meteorology of Kampong Speu Province
The grant will be used to rehabilitate an existing dam and two canals in Samrong Tong district, Kampong Speu province. We expect the project to benefit more than 3,300 local people by ensuring a stable water supply for livelihoods and agricultural activities.
Since 1991, the Government of Japan has provided more than $ 64 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement 651 Kusano projects throughout Cambodia.
According to Mikami Masahiro, since 1991, in addition to large-scale loans and grants, the Japanese government has been implementing the Kusano program in Cambodia to directly assist local authorities and NGOs in implementing its projects. They.
Kusano means "grass root" in Japanese. In the last 30 years, the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia has contributed to more than 650 projects through the Kusano Foundation Grant.
Co-Investigating Judges order the seizure of Case 003, filed by Meas Muth and placed in the archives
(Phnom Penh): The Co-Investigating Judges of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) on Monday 20 December 2021 issued a decision to seal Case 003 with Meas Muth in custody Message.
This decision was made after the Supreme Court Chamber on 17 December 2021 rejected the International Co-Prosecutor's request to send Case 003 against Meas Muth to trial.
The two Co-Investigating Judges, National Judge You Bunleng and International Co-Investigating Judge Michael Bolander, stated: "On 17 December 2021, the Supreme Court Chamber declared the case against Meas Muth Finished. However, the Chamber did not formally order itself to seal and archive the case.
"Based on these reasons, we order that Case 003 be sealed and archived." This is stated by the Co-Investigating Judges in the decision.
It should be recalled that on 17 December 2021, by a unanimous vote, the Supreme Court Chamber ruled that "the International Co-Prosecutor's Appeal on the Pre-Trial Chamber's failure to refer Case 003 to trial" Clearance as required by the ECCC Legal Framework "is acceptable in the interests of justice.
The application is considered by a majority chamber that provides clarity and legal certainty and:
1. Rejected the perception that the Pre-Trial Chamber had unanimously upheld the validity of the referral to the trial against Meas Muth in consideration of its appeal against the Closing Order.
2. Confirm that the Pre-Trial Chamber has completed Case 003 when the Trial Chamber has issued its deliberations.
